","The third law of thermodynamics states that the entropy of a perfect crystal at absolute zero is zero. This principle implies that the entropy of imperfect crystals at absolute zero is always greater than zero. This is because the entropy of an system is related to the number of possible microstates (micropositions) that correspond to the same macroscopic state. A perfect crystal at absolute zero has only one possible microstate (all atoms in their lowest vibrational states and in a perfect arrangement), leading to zero entropy.

