To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔS = ΔS_products - ΔS_reactants

where ΔS_products and ΔS_reactants are the standard entropies of the products and reactants, respectively.

The reaction is:

3CO(g) + 5O2(g) → 3CO2(g)

We need the standard entropies (S°) at 298 K for CO, O2, and CO2. From standard thermodynamic tables, we find:

S°(CO) = 227.3 J/mol·K
S°(O2) = 205.1 J/mol·K
S°(CO2) = 238.1 J/mol·K

Now we can calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = (3 * S°(CO2)) - (3 * S°(CO) + 5 * S°(O2))
ΔS = (3 * 238.1 J/mol·K) - (3 * 227.3 J/mol·K + 5 * 205.1 J/mol·K)
ΔS = 714.3 J/mol·K - (681.9 J/mol·K + 1025.5 J/mol·K)
ΔS = 714.3 J/mol·K - 1707.4 J/mol·K
ΔS = -993.1 J/mol·K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -993.1 J/mol·K.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

