\]
where \( n \) is the number of trials (12 flips), \( n' \) is the number of heads (9, 10, or 12), and \( p = \frac{1}{2} \) is the probability of getting a head in a single flip.

The probability that we get exactly \( n' \) heads in 12 flips is:
\[
P(X = n') = \binom{12}{n'} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{n'} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12-n'} = \binom{12}{n'} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12}
\]

To find the probability that we get at least 9 heads, we sum the probabilities for 9, 10, 11, and 12 heads:
\[
P(X \geq 9) = P(X=9) + P(X=10) + P(X=11) + P(X=12)
\]
\[
P(X \geq 9) = \binom{12}{9} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12} + \binom{12}{10} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12} + \binom{12}{11} \left( \frac{1}{ 2} \right)^{12} + \binom{12}{12} \left( \frac{1}{ 2} \right)^{12}
