","To solve this problem, we need to use the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a significant fraction of the speed of light relative to an observer. The formula for time dilation is:

t' = t / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where t' is the time measured by the observer on Earth, t is the time measured by the clock on the spaceship, and v is the speed of the spaceship relative to Earth.

In this case, we are given that the clock in the clock's time is 3 years (t = 3 years) when the ship returns to Earth. We are given that the spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c (v = 0.6c). We can now calculate the time measured on Earth (t'):

t' = 3 / sqrt(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
t' = 3 / sqrt(1 - 0.36)
t' = 3 / sqrt(0.64)
t' = 3 / 0.8
t' = 3.75 years

Now, the question is about the clock in the cargo base. Since both clocks are on board the ship, they will experience the same time dilation effect. Therefore, the time measured by the clock in the cargo bay will be the same as the time measured by the clock in the cockpit, which is 3 years.

However, the question asks for the time shown on the clock in the cargo base when it is compared to a clock on Earth. This is the same as the time measured by the clock on the spaceship as measured by the Earth observer, which we calculated as 3.75 years.

