To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction, we first need to determine the balanced chemical equation for the reaction between hydrogen and oxygen to form water:

\[ 2 \text{H}_2(g) + \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) \]

The change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction (\(\Delta G_{\text{rxn}}\)) can be calculated using the following equation:

\[ \Delta G_{\text{rxn}} = \sum \Delta G_f(\text{products}) - \sum \Delta G_f(\text{reactants}) \]

Given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation for water (\(\Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O})\)) is -237.1 kJ/mol, we can calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction:

\[ \Delta G_{\text{rxn}} = 2 \times \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}) - (2 \times \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2) + \1 \times \Delta G_f(\text{O}_2)) \]

The standard Gibbs free energy of formation for hydrogen (\(\Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\))) is 0 kJ/mol, and the standard Gibbs free energy of formation for oxygen (\(\Delta G_f(\text{O}_2\))) is 0 kJ/mol.

Thus, the calculation becomes:

\[ \Delta G_{\text{rxn}} = 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) - (2 \times 0 \text{ kJ/mol} + 1 \times 0 \text{ kJ/mol}) \]

\[ \Delta G_{\text{rxn}} = -474.2 \text{ kJ/mol} - 0 \text{ kJ/mol} \]

\[ \Delta G_{\text{rxn}} = -474.2 \text{ kJ/mol} \]

