","The lifetime of a particle is related to its width \(\Gamma\) by the formula:

\[
\Gamma = \frac{\hbar}{\tau}
\]

where \(\Gamma\) is the width, \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)), and \(\tau\) is the lifetime. The width \(\Gamma\) is related to the width of the energy spectrum of the particle, which is related to the uncertainty in the energy \(\Delta E\).

The uncertainty principle states:

\[
\Delta E \Delta t \geq \hbar
\]

For a short-lived particle, the lifetime \(\tau\) is related to the energy spread \(\Delta E\) as:

\[
\Delta E \approx \Gamma = \frac{\hbar}{\tau}
\]

Substituting \(\hbar = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\) and \(\tau = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{s}\):

\[
\Gamma = \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{2.0 \times 10^{-23}} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{J}
\]

Convert \(\Gamma\) to MeV:

\[
\Gamma = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{J} \times \frac{1 \, \text{eV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}} \times \frac{1 \, \text{MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^6 \, \text{eV}} \approx 3.29 \, \text{MeV}
\]

Thus, the width of the energy spectrum is \(\Delta E \approx 3.29 \, \text{MeV}\). This means any measurement of the rest energy of the \(\Sigma^+\) particle will fluctuate around the rest energy of 1385 MeV with a typical uncertainty of \(\pm 3.29 \, \text{MeV}\). 

However, experimental measurements are subject to experimental uncertainties, which may be larger or smaller than this theoretical uncertainty. For practice, the theoretical uncertainty is:

\[
\boxed{1385 \pm 3.29 \, \text{MeV}}
