4. The breakdown current \( I_{CBO} \) is given by the given \( V_{CBO} \).
5. The base-base capacitance is negligible.
6. The base-collector resistance \( R_{CB} \) is negligible.
7. The breakdown current \( I_{CBO} \) is independent of the input voltage \( V_{CB} \).
8. The base-base current \( I_B = 0 \) ensures that the base is open and no current flows into the base.

The breakdown current \( I_{CBO} \) occurs when the breakdown voltage \( V_{CBO} \) is reached. At this point, the depletion width in the base-collector junction becomes large, and the depletion region extends into the base.

The breakdown current \( I_{CCO} \) is the current through the collector-collector junction, which is the same as \( I_{CBO} \) in this case.

The breakdown voltage \( V_{CEO} \) is the voltage across the collector-emitter junction when the collector-base current \( I_{CC} \) equals the breakdown current \( I_{CBO} \).

The emitter-base junction is forward biased, and the collector-base junction is reverse biased. The voltage \( V_{CEO} \) is the sum of the voltage drop across the emitter-base junction \( V_{BE} \) and the voltage drop across the collector-base junction \( V_{CB} \). Thus, \( V_{CEO} = V_{BE} + V_{CB} \).

